From 6666ea27d83d92b5e9e4c0f5be60694de87c30da Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Viet Hung Nguyen <hvn@calif.io>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 22:57:18 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] feat: Add allocation-reusing value decoding

The generic `Reader` builds a fresh `Value` tree for every datum. Each
record allocates a new vector, clones every field name, and recursively
allocates its allocation-bearing children, so a caller decoding many
records of the same shape pays for that shape over and over. In an
allocation-heavy fixture, allocator and free functions accounted for
about 34% of exclusive sampled CPU time.

Add a decoding path that writes into caller-owned storage and reuses
what is already there:

  Reader::read_into(&mut Value) -> AvroResult<bool>
  GenericDatumReader::read_value_into(&mut R, &mut Value)
  OwnedGenericDatumReader, an owning variant of GenericDatumReader that
  resolves schema references once, for use in a long-lived reader

Records, arrays, unions, strings, bytes, fixed values, and enums reuse
compatible existing allocations. Maps, `Decimal`, `BigDecimal`, and
`Uuid` own representations with no public in-place decode API and fall
back to the owned decode path, as does reader-schema resolution, which
consumes a `Value` and can change its shape.

This is one decode path, not two. `Block::read_next`,
`GenericDatumReader::read_value`, and
`OwnedGenericDatumReader::read_value` delegate to the `_into` forms
with a fresh `Value::Null`, which finds no allocations to reuse and so
costs what it always did. That keeps block bookkeeping and error behavior
from drifting between the two forms, and it means the existing test
suite exercises the new decoder.

The per-datum allocation budget and the recursion depth limit tracked
by `DecodeContext` apply to the new path exactly as to the existing
one. The budget is charged by decoded size rather than by net-new
allocation, so both paths accept and reject the same inputs.

The new methods are additive: no existing signature or behavior
changes.

The included criterion bench (a record of strings, bytes, and an array
of records inside a nullable union) reads 10,000 records in 8.91 ms
through the iterator and 3.91 ms through `read_into` (2.3x) on one
aarch64 test machine. On workloads with deeper nesting the gap grows:
an eight-level nested fixture went from 553k to 1,699k records/s
(3.07x), and a 2,000-entry, 20-column Iceberg v2 manifest, where each
metrics map is an `array<record{key,value}>`, went from 85.5k to
388.2k entries/s (4.51x), with allocation operations for the whole
file falling from 902,003 to 455.
